1 STARTER Concepts
******************

Now that we’ve covered the "why," I want to dive into the "how".  Let’s
start with the name.  STARTER is an acronym for _Simple distribuTed
smART conTrollER_, and every letter reflects a core design principle.

Simple
        * Adding a new module is as easy as powering it on.
        * You don't need to be a senior dev to manage it; the control
          logic uses a near-natural language that anyone can understand.

distribuTed
        * Every component operates over the network.  It’s designed to
          be lightweight, running smoothly even on low-spec, legacy
          hardware.

smART
        * Services connect and disconnect on the fly, with rules applied
          automatically in real-time.
        * It doesn't just follow a schedule; it reacts to environmental
          changes.
        * It allows us to translate our specific will into action using
          a near-human syntax.

conTrollER
        * Through our custom rules, it acts as the "brain," driving
          every peripheral exactly as instructed.

I chose this name because I wanted it to be clear that this core is just
the _starting point_.  Any user can download it, plug in their own
services, and begin coordinating them.  Currently, my setup runs on a
single computer managing a mix of services.  However, the underlying
architecture is generic: it can be applied virtually anywhere you need
to define the logic: _"When these conditions are met, react this way"_.

