2.1 A Quick Disclaimer
======================

Before we dive in, it’s important to remember that STARTER is, first and
foremost, a "Core".  While the included examples are great for practice
and getting a feel for the logic, "the real deal" happens when you start
plugging in your own services and defining your unique rules.  Think of
the Core as the conductor and the services as the orchestra; you’ll need
to bring your own instruments to the stage.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: System Preparation,  Next: The Rules File: Where the Magic Happens,  Prev: A Quick Disclaimer,  Up: Using STARTER: From Theory to Practice

2.2 System Preparation
======================

STARTER is designed to run on a full GNU/Linux environment.

For the sake of efficiency (and, let’s be honest, a bit of developer
"laziness"), I developed the system on a _Debian-based_ distribution.
Because of this, the instructions below are tailored for that
environment.  If you are using a different distro, the core concepts
remain the same, but you might need to adapt some specific commands or
package names to fit your setup.

