File: starter-core.info,  Node: Dependencies,  Next: Database Initialization,  Up: System Preparation

2.2.1 Dependencies
------------------

To compile STARTER, you’ll need to prepare your environment.  The system
relies on several core libraries for networking and data management.

Required packages:
        * 'glib-2.0' and 'gio-2.0' (The backbone for system
          abstractions)
        * 'avahi-client' and 'avahi-glib' (For zero-configuration
          networking/service discovery)
        * 'libmysqlclient-dev' (To enable database connectivity)
Optional (but recommended):
        * 'flex', 'bison', and 'makeinfo' (Useful for specific build
          tasks or documentation)


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Database Initialization,  Next: Download and Installation,  Prev: Dependencies,  Up: System Preparation

2.2.2 Database Initialization
-----------------------------

Before launching the Core, you need a place for it to store its
"memory."  You must create a MariaDB/MySQL database—either locally or on
a remote server—named 'STARTER'.

The database requires three specific tables: 'labels', 'history', and
'stats'.  You can find the exact SQL schema and creation scripts in
**Appendix XXX**.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Download and Installation,  Next: Configuration,  Prev: Database Initialization,  Up: System Preparation

2.2.3 Download and Installation
-------------------------------

STARTER follows the standard GNU packaging conventions and utilizes
'autotools'.  If you’ve ever compiled software on Linux, you’ll feel
right at home.  Run the following commands from your terminal:

     cd STARTER-DIRECTORY
     ./configure
     make
     sudo make install

The Core will be installed (typically under '/usr/local') and
automatically configured as a 'systemd' service.  Note that the service
is not enabled for auto-boot by default—we’ll leave that decision to you
once everything is configured.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Configuration,  Next: Where to go from here?,  Prev: Download and Installation,  Up: System Preparation

2.2.4 Configuration
-------------------

The final step is to point STARTER to your database.  Instead of messy
config files, STARTER uses 'gsettings' for a clean, integrated
configuration.  Use the command line to set your credentials:

     gsettings set org.starter.core db-host 'YOUR_IP_OR_HOSTNAME'
     gsettings set org.starter.core db-port PORT
     gsettings set org.starter.core db-user 'YOUR_USERNAME'
     gsettings set org.starter.core db-pass 'YOUR_PASSWORD'

     Note: Ensure the database user has full Read/Write permissions.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Where to go from here?,  Prev: Configuration,  Up: System Preparation

2.2.5 Where to go from here?
----------------------------

Your Core is now standing by, ready for orders.  What happens next
depends entirely on your objective—whether it's home automation,
industrial control, or building a smart city.

  1. Install your services: Deploy the specific modules you need for
     your project.
  2. Fire them up: Once the services are live, they will automatically
     announce themselves to the Core.
  3. Define the Logic: Write your rules and watch the system transform
     from a collection of parts into an intelligent, autonomous
     environment.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Rules File: Where the Magic Happens,  Prev: System Preparation,  Up: Using STARTER: From Theory to Practice

2.3 The Rules File: Where the Magic Happens
===========================================

The rules file is the true heart of your system.  This is where your
abstract ideas turn into reality: "_If it rains, close the shutters_",
or "_If it’s a sunny day, run the irrigation at sunset_".

By default, this file is located at 'INSTALLDIR/etc/starter/rules.conf'.
To help you master the STARTER syntax, we’ll use the provided example
services.  Let’s open the file and write our very first logic block:

     beep:status {
         SET TRUE WHEN time:hour == 7 && time:minute == 0;
         SET FALSE WHEN TRUE;
     }

If you now launch 'starter-core', 'time-example', and 'beep-example',
you’ve just built an alarm clock that rings every morning at 7:00 for
exactly one minute.  Simple, right?  But let’s look under the hood to
see how it actually works.

