2.3.6.1 Constant Values and Types
.................................

All numerical constants in STARTER are handled as _double-precision
floating-point numbers_.  Whether you're comparing TIME:HOUR to '7' or
calculating a precise voltage offset, the system provides high
precision.  While specific limits depend on your hardware, even on a
32-bit machine, the margin for error is negligible for automation
purposes.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Boolean Logic: Everything is a Number,  Next: Comparison and Logical Operators,  Prev: Constant Values and Types,  Up: Expressions: The Engine of STARTER

2.3.6.2 Boolean Logic: Everything is a Number
.............................................

In STARTER, 'TRUE' and 'FALSE' are simply aliases for constants:

'FALSE'
     Is exactly '0'.
'TRUE'
     Is exactly '1'.

However, the system evaluates _any non-zero value as 'TRUE'_.  This is a
classic programmer's concept, but it's vital for clever automation.  For
example:

     beep:status {
         SET TRUE WHEN time:minute;
         SET FALSE WHEN TRUE;
     }

_Why would this make the computer beep almost all the time?_  Because
TIME:MINUTE is only '0' at the very start of the hour.  For every other
minute (1 through 59), the value is non-zero, which the system
interprets as 'TRUE'.  The beep only stops for that first minute of the
hour!


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Comparison and Logical Operators,  Next: Mathematical Operators,  Prev: Boolean Logic: Everything is a Number,  Up: Expressions: The Engine of STARTER

2.3.6.3 Comparison and Logical Operators
........................................

You can relate different expressions using standard operators:

Operator               Description
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
'==', '!='             Equal to / Not equal to
'<', '<='              Less than / Less than or equal to
'>', '>='              Greater than / Greater than or equal to
'&&'                   _AND_: True only if both expressions are true
'||'                   _OR_: True if at least one expression is true

You can use _parentheses_ '()' to group sub-expressions and control the
evaluation order.  Want an alarm that rings at 7:00 AM _OR_ whenever oil
prices drop?

     SET TRUE WHEN (time:hour == 7 && time:minute == 0) || oil:price < 100;


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Mathematical Operators,  Next: Built-in Functions,  Prev: Comparison and Logical Operators,  Up: Expressions: The Engine of STARTER

2.3.6.4 Mathematical Operators
..............................

STARTER isn't just for logic; it’s for math.  It supports '+', '-', '*',
'/', and the unary minus '-'.  Standard mathematical precedence applies
(multiplication/division before addition/subtraction), but you can use
parentheses to force your own order.

'2 * 1 + 4 = 6'
'2 * (1 + 4) = 10'
'time:minute + (time:hour * 60) = _Total minutes since midnight._'


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Built-in Functions,  Prev: Mathematical Operators,  Up: Expressions: The Engine of STARTER

2.3.6.5 Built-in Functions
..........................

To simplify complex rules, STARTER provides a set of powerful functions:

'MIN(arg1, arg2, ...)'
     Returns the lowest value in a list.
'MAX(arg1, arg2, ...)'
     Returns the highest value in a list.
'ROUND(value)'
     Rounds to the nearest whole integer.
'LIMIT(value, min, max)'
     Clamps a value.  If it goes below MIN, it returns MIN; if above
     MAX, it returns MAX.
'CHANGED(id)'
     This is a special one.  It returns '1' ('TRUE') only if the value
     of that specific ID has changed since the last evaluation.  It's
     perfect for triggering actions only on a fresh update.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Mechanics of Evaluation: Snapshots vs. Live Streams,  Next: Preprocessing: The Secret Weapon,  Prev: Expressions: The Engine of STARTER,  Up: The Rules File: Where the Magic Happens

2.3.7 The Mechanics of Evaluation: Snapshots vs. Live Streams
-------------------------------------------------------------

Understanding how STARTER "thinks" when it encounters an expression is
the difference between a static setup and a truly fluid system.  Let's
break down a simple logic gate:

     time:second == 0;

To evaluate this, the system doesn't just look at a stored number; it
polls the value of TIME:SECOND at the exact millisecond the calculation
is triggered.  If it catches the clock at the top of the minute, the
result is 'TRUE'.  Otherwise, it’s 'FALSE'.

But here’s where it gets interesting: _Variables_.

     VAR second;
     second = time:second;

If you’re coming from traditional programming, you might expect SECOND
to hold a static "snapshot" of the time when that line was executed.
_In STARTER, it doesn't_.  By default, a variable acts as a _Live
Reference_.  Using SECOND or TIME:SECOND in your rules is functionally
the same—it’s a dynamic link that updates in real-time as the clock
ticks.

* Menu:

* The EVAL Function: Killing the Link::
* Why This Matters: Architectural Elegance::
* The Boot-Time Paradox::


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The EVAL Function: Killing the Link,  Next: Why This Matters: Architectural Elegance,  Up: The Mechanics of Evaluation: Snapshots vs. Live Streams

2.3.7.1 The EVAL Function: Killing the Link
...........................................

Sometimes, you don't want a live stream; you want to "freeze" a moment
in time.  This is where the _'EVAL()'_ function comes into play.  It
forces the system to calculate the final result of an expression and
store it as a static constant.

     The "Cheat Sheet": 'value = 1;'
          Hard-coded Constant.
     'value = time:hour;'
          _Dynamic Reference_ (The value evolves with the hour).
     'value = EVAL(time:hour);'
          _Frozen Value_ (Locks the hour at the moment of assignment).
     'value = 1 + time:hour;'
          _Dynamic Calculation_ (The result updates as time moves).
     'value = EVAL(1 + time:hour);'
          _Static Result_ (Calculates the sum once and stores the
          total).


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Why This Matters: Architectural Elegance,  Next: The Boot-Time Paradox,  Prev: The EVAL Function: Killing the Link,  Up: The Mechanics of Evaluation: Snapshots vs. Live Streams

2.3.7.2 Why This Matters: Architectural Elegance
................................................

This "Reference-first" approach allows you to build a sophisticated,
human-readable logic tree without repeating yourself:

     VAR its_seven_o_clock = (time:hour == 7 && time:minute == 0);

     beep:status {
         SET TRUE WHEN its_seven_o_clock;
         SET FALSE WHEN TRUE;
     }

By using a reference (ITS_SEVEN_O_CLOCK), you’ve created an _Alias_.
Every time STARTER checks the BEEP:STATUS, it follows that alias back to
the original time logic.  It’s clean, it’s abstract, and it’s incredibly
powerful.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Boot-Time Paradox,  Prev: Why This Matters: Architectural Elegance,  Up: The Mechanics of Evaluation: Snapshots vs. Live Streams

2.3.7.3 The Boot-Time Paradox
.............................

A quick "gotcha" for the pros:

     VAR startup_hour = EVAL(time:hour);

You might intend to capture the exact hour the system starts.  However,
at the microsecond of boot-up, the ST:TIME service might still be
authenticating on the network.  If the input doesn't exist yet, 'EVAL'
can't do its job.  Don't worry—STARTER handles this gracefully by
performing the evaluation as soon as the required service makes its
first appearance on the grid.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Preprocessing: The Secret Weapon,  Next: Command Line Options: Taking the Reins,  Prev: The Mechanics of Evaluation: Snapshots vs. Live Streams,  Up: The Rules File: Where the Magic Happens

2.3.8 Preprocessing: The Secret Weapon
--------------------------------------

The final layer of the STARTER language is _Preprocessing_.  Before the
Core ever reads your rules, the file is passed through an external
program that modifies the text on the fly.  What STARTER actually
executes is the _output_ of that program.

If you’ve spent time in the trenches with C or C++, this concept will
feel familiar—though STARTER’s implementation is even more flexible.  If
you’re not a programmer, think of it this way: the preprocessor is like
an automated editor that prepares your "manuscript" before the publisher
(the Core) sees it.

* Menu:

* Power Unleashed: The M4 Default::
* Swapping the Engine::


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Power Unleashed: The M4 Default,  Next: Swapping the Engine,  Up: Preprocessing: The Secret Weapon

2.3.8.1 Power Unleashed: The M4 Default
.......................................

By default, STARTER uses 'm4', the powerhouse preprocessor of the GNU
system.  It is incredibly deep—too deep for a single manual—but it
allows for advanced maneuvers like macros, file inclusions, and
conditional text generation.

While I won't turn you into an 'm4' expert here, you need to know it’s
there because it gives you the ability to manage massive, complex sets
of rules without repeating yourself.  If you’re curious, the official
'm4' manual is a rabbit hole worth exploring.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Swapping the Engine,  Prev: Power Unleashed: The M4 Default,  Up: Preprocessing: The Secret Weapon

2.3.8.2 Swapping the Engine
...........................

The beauty of STARTER’s philosophy is that you aren't locked in.  Don't
like 'm4'?  You can swap the preprocessor for any command-line tool you
prefer.

Returning to our 'gsettings' configuration, you can change the
preprocessor with a single command.  For example, if you want to bypass
preprocessing entirely and just read the file "as-is," you can use the
standard 'cat' command:

     gsettings set org.starter.core rules-preprocessor 'cat {F}'

In this syntax, '{F}' is a placeholder that STARTER automatically
replaces with the path to your rules file.  This flexibility means you
could even use a Python script, a custom parser, or a simple text filter
as your preprocessor.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Command Line Options: Taking the Reins,  Prev: Preprocessing: The Secret Weapon,  Up: The Rules File: Where the Magic Happens

2.3.9 Command Line Options: Taking the Reins
--------------------------------------------

While STARTER is designed to run quietly in the background as a system
service, there are times—especially during debugging or initial
setup—when you’ll want to launch it manually.

When you run STARTER from the terminal, you can pass several options to
override the default system settings.  This is incredibly useful for
testing a new set of rules or increasing the log detail without touching
your permanent configuration.

The supported options are:

Option  Long option            Description
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
'-h'    '--help'               Show all available help options and exit.
'-n'    '--no-discovery'       Disable the automatic discovery of
                               network devices/services.
'-m'    '--service-match'      Regular expression to filter recognized
                               services.  Default: ''^(STARTER:|ST:).*''
'-R'    '--rules'              Specify a custom path to a rules file
                               (overrides the default 'rules.conf').
'-P'    '--rules-preprocessor' Define a specific command to preprocess
                               the rules file on the fly.
'-V'    '--verbosity'          Adjust the detail of the output logs
                               (essential for troubleshooting).
'-v'    '--version'            Print the current version of the Core and
                               exit.

* Menu:

* Advanced Orchestration::
* The Override Hierarchy::


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Advanced Orchestration,  Next: The Override Hierarchy,  Up: Command Line Options: Taking the Reins

2.3.9.1 Advanced Orchestration: Segmenting the System
.....................................................

The new '--service-match' (or '-m') option is a powerful tool for
advanced users who wish to split their automation into multiple
independent domains.

By default, the Core listens to any service starting with 'STARTER:' or
'ST:'.  However, by modifying this regular expression, you can run
multiple Cores on the same network without interference.  Each Core will
only "see" and manage services whose names match its specific pattern.

For example, you could have:
   * A Core managing only critical infrastructure: 'starter
     --service-match '^ST:CRITICAL:.*''
   * A Core managing leisure or lab services: 'starter --service-match
     '^ST:LAB:.*''

This segmentation ensures that a failure or a configuration error in one
domain (like the lab) does not affect the stability of another (like the
main heating system).


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Override Hierarchy,  Prev: Advanced Orchestration,  Up: Command Line Options: Taking the Reins

2.3.9.2 The Override Hierarchy
..............................

Remember: _Command-line arguments are king_.  If you have a database
host set via 'gsettings' but specify a different rule path or
service-match via the terminal, the command-line flag will always take
precedence.  This allows you to experiment with "sandbox" configurations
without breaking your main production setup.

3 System Architecture: Under the Hood
*************************************

Now that you’ve mastered the language and the installation, it’s time to
look at the "nervous system" of STARTER.  Understanding how the Core
communicates with its services is key to building a truly robust
automation network.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: A Truly Distributed Ecosystem

3.1 A Truly Distributed Ecosystem
=================================

As we touched upon in the introduction, STARTER is a _distributed
system_.  It isn't tied to a single CPU or a single box.

_Single-Machine Mode_
     For a home setup, one machine can handle everything.
_Large-Scale Deployment_
     In a smart city or industrial scenario, you can spread the workload
     across dozens of low-power machines.

The architecture follows a _Client/Server principle_.  Each service that
manages I/O acts as a _Server_, while the Core acts as the _Client_.
You aren't limited to one client; you can deploy multiple Cores across a
network, each handling a specific subset of services and logic.

* Menu:

* ZeroConf: Plug & Play Reality::
* Communication via D-Bus RPC::
* The Operational Loop::


File: starter-core.info,  Node: ZeroConf: Plug & Play Reality,  Next: Communication via D-Bus RPC,  Up: A Truly Distributed Ecosystem

3.1.1 ZeroConf: Plug & Play Reality
-----------------------------------

One of the most frustrating parts of automation is manual network
configuration.  STARTER eliminates this via _ZeroConf_.

The Core constantly listens for _mDNS (Multicast DNS)_ events.  The
moment a new service is powered on and publishes itself on the network,
the Core recognizes it, initiates a connection, and synchronizes
automatically.  No IP addresses to type, no config files to update.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Communication via D-Bus RPC,  Next: The Operational Loop,  Prev: ZeroConf: Plug & Play Reality,  Up: A Truly Distributed Ecosystem

3.1.2 Communication via D-Bus RPC
---------------------------------

The "dialogue" between clients and servers happens over the _D-Bus TCP
protocol_.  This Remote Procedure Call (RPC) mechanism allows for
high-speed, reliable data exchange.

When a service is discovered, the Core performs a "handshake" using
three primary methods:

'getInputs'
     The Core requests a list of all available inputs from the service.
     Once received, it "hooks" into specific _'*-changed' signals_.
     This ensures that the moment a sensor value shifts at the service
     level, the Core is notified instantly.

'getOutputs'
     Similar to inputs, the Core fetches the array of available outputs.
     It hooks the change signals here as well, keeping the Core’s
     internal map perfectly synchronized with the service’s real-world
     state.

'getStats'
     Finally, the Core retrieves the service’s internal statistics.
     Like the I/O, these are monitored via signals so that any
     diagnostic or performance data is updated in real-time.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Operational Loop,  Prev: Communication via D-Bus RPC,  Up: A Truly Distributed Ecosystem

3.1.3 The Operational Loop
--------------------------

Once the handshake is complete, the Client and Server are fully linked.
Here is the lifecycle of a single action:

_Change_
     A service updates an Input (e.g., a thermometer reads a new
     temperature).
_Notification_
     The service fires a signal; the Core receives it immediately.
_Processing_
     The Core re-evaluates all rules related to that Input.
_Action_
     If a rule triggers a change in an Output, the Core calls the
     'SetOutput' method on the service.
_Alignment_
     The service receives the command and adjusts the physical or
     abstract hardware to match the requested value.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The starter-core Library: Building Your Own Ecosystem,  Next: Conclusions: The World is Your Input,  Up: Top

4 The starter-core Library: Building Your Own Ecosystem
*******************************************************

The heavy lifting—networking, D-Bus signaling, and service discovery—is
already baked into the library.  This means you can focus on your
specific logic while the library handles the "plumbing."

Let's dive into the anatomy of a service.  We’ll build a template for
your first service in just a few lines of C.

* Menu:

* The Skeleton: first-service.c::
* Adding Substance: Inputs and Outputs::
* Signals: The Reactive Core::
* The Grand Finale: Bridging Code and Logic::


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Skeleton: first-service.c,  Next: Adding Substance: Inputs and Outputs,  Up: The starter-core Library: Building Your Own Ecosystem

4.1 The Skeleton: 'first-service.c'
===================================

     #include <stdlib.h>
     #include <glib.h>

     #include <starter/mdns.h>
     #include <starter/server.h>
     #include <starter/input.h>
     #include <starter/output.h>

     static GPtrArray *inputs = NULL;
     static GPtrArray *outputs = NULL;
     static STVersion version = { 0, 1, 0 };

     int main(int argc, char *argv[])
     {
       GMainLoop *gloop = NULL;
       STServer *server;
       STMDNS *mdns;
       GError *error = NULL;

       gloop = g_main_loop_new(NULL, FALSE);

       // Initializing I/O containers
       inputs = g_ptr_array_new_full(0, g_object_unref);
       outputs = g_ptr_array_new_full(0, g_object_unref);

       // Creating the Server instance
       server = st_server_new("ST:MyFirstService", &version, inputs, outputs);
       st_server_start(server, 0, &error);

       // Publishing the service via ZeroConf (mDNS)
       mdns = st_mdns_new();
       st_mdns_publish_service(mdns, st_server_get_name(server), "_dbus._tcp", st_server_get_port(server));

       g_main_loop_run(gloop);

       return EXIT_SUCCESS;
     }

* Menu:

* Compiling and Running::
* Breaking Down the Code::


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Compiling and Running,  Next: Breaking Down the Code,  Up: The Skeleton: first-service.c

4.1.1 Compiling and Running
---------------------------

To turn this code into a binary, use 'gcc' along with 'pkg-config' to
automatically pull in the necessary flags and libraries:

     gcc -o my-first-service my-first-service.c `pkg-config --cflags --libs starter-core`

When you run it, it might look like it's doing nothing.  But by enabling
debug logs, you can watch the handshake happen in real-time:

     export G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=libstarter-core
     ./my-first-service

If the Core is already running, you'll see your service "answering" the
Core's queries:

     ... handle method call GetInputs
     ... handle method call GetOutputs
     ... handle method call GetStats


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Breaking Down the Code,  Prev: Compiling and Running,  Up: The Skeleton: first-service.c

4.1.2 Breaking Down the Code
----------------------------

'st_server_new(...)'
     This initializes your service.  You provide a unique name (like
     'ST:MyFirstService'), a version, and the I/O arrays.  A key feature
     here is _shared memory_: the library doesn't copy your inputs and
     outputs; it shares them.  If your code updates a sensor value, the
     library knows immediately.  If the Core sends a command to an
     output, your code is notified.
'st_server_start(server, 0, ...)'
     This starts the D-Bus server on a local port.  By passing '0', the
     library automatically finds the first available port—no more manual
     port management.
'st_mdns_new()'
'st_mdns_publish_service(...)'
     This is where the "magic" happens.  The first call sets up the
     discovery engine; the second broadcasts your service's name and
     port to the network.  This is the broadcast that the Core hears,
     triggering the automatic connection.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Adding Substance: Inputs and Outputs,  Next: Signals: The Reactive Core,  Prev: The Skeleton: first-service.c,  Up: The starter-core Library: Building Your Own Ecosystem

4.2 Adding Substance: Inputs and Outputs
========================================

In the STARTER ecosystem, a service exists to share data.  To do that,
we need to populate our INPUTS and OUTPUTS arrays.  Let’s upgrade our
code to version 0.2.0:

     // ... headers as before ...

     int main(int argc, char *argv[])
     {
       // ... boilerplate initialization ...

       // Defining a new Input
       inputs = g_ptr_array_new_full(0, g_object_unref);
       g_ptr_array_add(inputs, st_input_new("myFirstInput",
         "My first input", ST_UNIT_NULL, 0, 1, 1, 0, ST_IO_FLAG_NULL));

       // Defining a new Output
       outputs = g_ptr_array_new_full(0, g_object_unref);
       g_ptr_array_add(outputs, st_output_new("myFirstOutput",
         "My first output", ST_UNIT_NULL, 0, 1, 1, 0, ST_IO_FLAG_NULL));

       // ... server creation and mDNS publish ...
     }

* Menu:

* The Anatomy of an I/O Object::
* The Power of Flags: Customizing I/O Behavior::


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Anatomy of an I/O Object,  Next: The Power of Flags: Customizing I/O Behavior,  Up: Adding Substance: Inputs and Outputs

4.2.1 The Anatomy of an I/O Object
----------------------------------

To create these objects, we use the 'st_input_new' and 'st_output_new'
functions.  Their signatures are identical, but their roles in the
system logic are opposite.

     STInput * st_input_new(
         const gchar *name,         // Unique identifier (e.g., "temperature")
         const gchar *description,  // Human-readable description
         STUnit unit,               // Unit of measurement (Celsius, Percentage, etc.)
         gdouble min,               // Minimum allowed value
         gdouble max,               // Maximum allowed value
         gdouble step,              // Resolution/Step of the value
         gdouble val,               // Initial value
         guint64 flags              // Special behavior flags
     );


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Power of Flags: Customizing I/O Behavior,  Prev: The Anatomy of an I/O Object,  Up: Adding Substance: Inputs and Outputs

4.2.2 The Power of Flags: Customizing I/O Behavior
--------------------------------------------------

A critical, yet often overlooked, field in both 'STInput' and 'STOutput'
objects is the FLAGS field.  These bitwise flags allow you to modify how
the Core interacts with a specific data point, giving you the power to
bypass standard logic or optimize database performance on the fly.

The system defines the following flags:

'ST_IO_FLAG_NULL'
     No flags set.  The I/O behaves according to standard system
     defaults.

'ST_IO_FLAG_MANUAL_OVERRIDE'
     This is a "safety" flag for Outputs.  When set, the Core is
     prohibited from automatically changing the value.  This is
     essential for maintenance modes or when you need to take manual
     control of an actuator without the logic blocks interfering.

'ST_IO_FLAG_NO_HISTORY'
     Use this to keep your database lean.  If an Input or Output has
     this flag, the Core will process the data in real-time but will not
     write it to the history tables.  Perfect for high-frequency data
     that doesn't require long-term logging.

'ST_IO_FLAG_USR1'
'ST_IO_FLAG_USR2'
'ST_IO_FLAG_USR3'
'ST_IO_FLAG_USR4'
     These are reserved for you.  They carry no internal meaning for the
     STARTER Core, allowing you to implement custom logic filters or
     tagging within your own services.

By mastering these flags, you aren't just defining data; you are
defining the policy of that data within your distributed network.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Signals: The Reactive Core,  Next: The Grand Finale: Bridging Code and Logic,  Prev: Adding Substance: Inputs and Outputs,  Up: The starter-core Library: Building Your Own Ecosystem

4.3 Signals: The Reactive Core
==============================

These objects are derived from 'GObject', which means they are "chatty."
They communicate changes through signals.  Every time you modify a
property, the object alerts the system.

'changed'
     The "master" signal.  It fires whenever *any* property of the
     object changes.
'name-changed'
'descr-changed'
'unit-changed'
'min-changed'
'max-changed'
'step-changed'
'val-changed'
'flags-changed'
     These fire only when their specific property is updated.

For instance, if you update the MIN value of a sensor, both 'changed'
and 'min-changed' will be emitted.  This allows the Core (or other parts
of your code) to react only to the information that matters.

* Menu:

* Input vs. Output: What's the difference?::


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Input vs. Output: What's the difference?,  Up: Signals: The Reactive Core

4.3.1 Input vs. Output: What's the difference?
----------------------------------------------

If the parameters are identical, why do we have two different types?
'STInput'
     Represents data coming _from the service to the Core_ (e.g., a
     sensor reading).  The service "owns" the value; the Core consumes
     it.
'STOutput'
     Represents data coming _from the Core to the service_ (e.g., a
     command to turn on a light).  The Core "requests" a value; the
     service executes it.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Grand Finale: Bridging Code and Logic,  Prev: Signals: The Reactive Core,  Up: The starter-core Library: Building Your Own Ecosystem

4.4 The Grand Finale: Bridging Code and Logic
=============================================

An _Input_ is a stream of data to be read; an _Output_ is a command
waiting to be written.  Now that we’ve defined our I/O, let's stop
talking and start _acting_.

We’re going to evolve our service into a living entity.  We’ll implement
a _sensor simulation_ (a timer that increments a value) and a "callback"
that waits for the Core to bark an order.

* Menu:

* The Living Service: `first-service-v3.c`::
* The Rule: Closing the Loop::
* The Result: A Symphony of Data::


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Living Service: `first-service-v3.c`,  Next: The Rule: Closing the Loop,  Up: The Grand Finale: Bridging Code and Logic

4.4.1 The Living Service: 'first-service-v3.c'
----------------------------------------------

     #include <starter/mdns.h>
     #include <starter/server.h>
     #include <starter/input.h>
     #include <starter/output.h>

     static GPtrArray *inputs = NULL;
     static GPtrArray *outputs = NULL;
     static STVersion version = { 0, 3, 0 };

     // Simulation: Incrementing our virtual sensor every second
     static gboolean update_input (gpointer data) {
         STInput *input = ST_INPUT(data);
         gdouble val = st_input_get_val(input);

         val = (val < 100) ? val + 1 : 0;

         st_input_set_val(input, val, NULL); // This triggers the *-changed signal automatically
         return G_SOURCE_CONTINUE;
     }

     // Callback: This fires the moment the Core sends a command to our service
     static void on_output_changed(STOutput *out, gpointer data) {
         g_print("Action Triggered! Core set output to: %lf\n", st_output_get_val(out));
     }

     int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
         GMainLoop *gloop = g_main_loop_new(NULL, FALSE);
         STServer *server;
         STMDNS *mdns;

         inputs = g_ptr_array_new_full(0, g_object_unref);
         g_ptr_array_add(inputs, st_input_new("myFirstInput", "Virtual Sensor", ST_UNIT_NULL, 0, 100, 1, 0, ST_IO_FLAG_NULL));

         outputs = g_ptr_array_new_full(0, g_object_unref);
         g_ptr_array_add(outputs, st_output_new("myFirstOutput", "Actuator", ST_UNIT_NULL, 0, 1, 1, 0, ST_IO_FLAG_NULL));

         // CONNECTING THE SIGNAL: Tell the code to listen for "val-changed"
         g_signal_connect(outputs->pdata[0], "val-changed", G_CALLBACK(on_output_changed), NULL);

         server = st_server_new("ST:MyFirstService", &version, inputs, outputs);
         st_server_start(server, 0, NULL);
         mdns = st_mdns_new();
         st_mdns_publish_service(mdns, st_server_get_name(server), "_dbus._tcp", st_server_get_port(server));

         // Start the heartbeat: Update the input every 1 second
         g_timeout_add_seconds(1, update_input, inputs->pdata[0]);

         g_main_loop_run(gloop);
         return EXIT_SUCCESS;
     }


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Rule: Closing the Loop,  Next: The Result: A Symphony of Data,  Prev: The Living Service: `first-service-v3.c`,  Up: The Grand Finale: Bridging Code and Logic

4.4.2 The Rule: Closing the Loop
--------------------------------

With the service broadcasting and listening, we just need the "brain" to
make a decision.  Open your 'rules.conf' and drop this in:

     myFirstOutput {
         SET 1 WHEN myFirstInput > 50;
         SET 0 WHEN TRUE;
     }


File: starter-core.info,  Node: The Result: A Symphony of Data,  Prev: The Rule: Closing the Loop,  Up: The Grand Finale: Bridging Code and Logic

4.4.3 The Result: A Symphony of Data
------------------------------------

Here is what happens when you hit "Run":
_Discovery_
     Your service shouts its existence over mDNS; the Core hears it and
     hooks up via D-Bus.
_Telemetry_
     Your code starts counting.  Every second, the Core receives the
     updated 'myFirstInput'.
_Threshold_
     The moment that counter hits **51**, the Core’s rule evaluates to
     'TRUE'.
_Reaction_
     The Core calls 'SetOutput' on your service.  Your
     'on_output_changed' function fires, and you see the confirmation in
     your console.

_Congratulations_ You’ve just built a fully distributed, reactive
automation loop.  You’ve bridged the gap between raw C code and
high-level abstract logic.  This is no longer just a "beep example"—it's
the blueprint for an entire ecosystem.


