B.1 Error Handling: The 'STError' Domain
========================================

In a complex ecosystem where services communicate over the network via
D-Bus, error handling is not just an option—it is a necessity.  STARTER
uses the GLib 'GError' system to report failures in a standardized way.

* Menu:

* STError::
* Error Quarks and Macros::


File: starter-core.info,  Node: STError,  Next: Error Quarks and Macros,  Up: Error Handling

B.1.1 STError Enumeration
-------------------------

The 'STError' enumeration defines the specific error codes returned by
the library.  These errors are associated with the 'ST_ERROR' quark.

 -- Enumeration: STError
     This enumeration defines the failure modes that can occur during
     I/O operations or service communication.

     'ST_ERROR_INVALID_VALUE'
          The value provided for an operation is outside the allowed
          range (defined by MIN and MAX) or is incompatible with the
          expected 'STUnit'.

     'ST_ERROR_INVALID_RESPONSE'
          The remote service or the Core sent a response that does not
          match the expected D-Bus signature or contains malformed data.

     'ST_ERROR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED'
          The requested method or operation is recognized but not
          currently supported by the specific version of the service or
          the library.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Error Quarks and Macros,  Prev: STError,  Up: Error Handling

B.1.2 Error Quarks and Macros
-----------------------------

To work with GLib's error reporting system, STARTER defines a specific
error domain.

 -- Macro: ST_ERROR
     This macro expands to the result of 'st_error_quark()'.  It is used
     to identify the error domain when catching or throwing errors:

          if (error->domain == ST_ERROR) {
              /* Handle STARTER specific errors */
          }

 -- Function: GQuark st_error_quark (void)
     Returns a 'GQuark' that uniquely identifies the STARTER error
     domain.  This function is typically called via the 'ST_ERROR'
     macro.

B.2 API Definitions: Constants and Enumerations
===============================================

This section describes the foundational constants and types defined in
'starter/defs.h'.  These definitions are shared across the entire
ecosystem to ensure consistent behavior between services and the Core.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: I/O Flags,  Next: STUnit Enumeration

B.2.1 I/O Flags
---------------

Flags are used to modify the behavior of 'STInput' and 'STOutput'
objects.  They are defined as bitwise constants of type 'guint64'.

'ST_IO_FLAG_NULL'
     No flags set ('0x0').  Standard system behavior applies.

'ST_IO_FLAG_MANUAL_OVERRIDE'
     When set on an Output, it prevents the Core from automatically
     updating its value.  This is used for manual maintenance or safety
     locks.

'ST_IO_FLAG_NO_HISTORY'
     Disables database logging for the specific I/O. The Core processes
     changes in real-time but does not record them in the 'history'
     table.

'ST_IO_FLAG_USR1'
'ST_IO_FLAG_USR2'
'ST_IO_FLAG_USR3'
'ST_IO_FLAG_USR4'
     User-defined flags.  These have no internal meaning for the Core
     and can be used by developers for custom service-level logic.

'ST_IO_FLAG_USR_MASK'
     A bitmask ('0xF000000000000000LL') provided to easily isolate or
     clear all user-defined flags.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: STUnit Enumeration,  Next: Special Constants,  Prev: I/O Flags

B.2.2 STUnit Enumeration
------------------------

The 'STUnit' enumeration defines the measurement units supported by the
system.  Every Input and Output must be associated with a unit to ensure
proper data interpretation and visualization (e.g., in Grafana
dashboards).

 -- Enumeration: STUnit
     'ST_UNIT_NULL'
          No unit specified.
     'ST_UNIT_CELSIUS'
          Temperature in degrees Celsius.
     'ST_UNIT_PERCENT'
          Percentage values (0.0 to 100.0).
     'ST_UNIT_BAR'
          Pressure in bars.
     'ST_UNIT_ONOFF'
          Binary state representation.
     'ST_UNIT_ENUM'
          A discrete list of states.
     'ST_UNIT_V'
     'ST_UNIT_W'
     'ST_UNIT_VAR'
     'ST_UNIT_KW'
          Electrical units: Volts, Watts, Volt-Ampere Reactive, and
          Kilowatts.
     'ST_UNIT_WH'
     'ST_UNIT_KWH'
          Energy consumption units: Watt-hours and Kilowatt-hours.
     'ST_UNIT_SECONDS'
     'ST_UNIT_MINUTES'
     'ST_UNIT_HOURS'
          Time duration units.


File: starter-core.info,  Node: Special Constants,  Prev: STUnit Enumeration

